/**

 @page page_exahype_amr Adaptive mesh refinement

### Adding adaptive mesh refinement (AMR)

AMR is application- and numerics-specific. Therefore, the signatures of
the routines discussed below might change if you switch from Finite
Volumes to ADER-DG, e.g. The discussed principles however always are the
same.

By default, ExaHyPE solvers operate on a regular grid. To facilitate an
adaptive mesh, you have to ensure that the grid is allowed to span more
than one resolution, i.e. that @f$h_{\text{max}}>h_{\text{min}}@f$. This
is controlled through the minimum and maximum number of grid levels that
you pass to your `exahype2.Project`. As ExaHyPE operates with
three-partitioning, each additional level makes the mesh finer by a
factor of three, so

@f$h_{\text{min}} \leq \frac{1}{3} h_{\text{max}}@f$

is the coarsest setting that actually switches on adaptivity:

    exahype2_project = exahype2.Project(
        ...
        min_grid_levels=coarse_levels,
        max_grid_levels=coarse_levels + 1,  # at least one extra level
    )

Once AMR is, in principle, enabled, you still have to supplement a
refinement and/or coarsening criterion.

##### Supplementing the refinement criterion

On the Python side, dynamic adaptivity is requested through your solver's
`set_implementation()` call by handing in a refinement criterion:

    my_solver.set_implementation(
        ...
        refinement_criterion=exahype2.solvers.PDETerms.User_Defined_Implementation,
    )

Once you rerun your Python script, ExaHyPE expects your solver to provide a
new routine `refinementCriterion()`. If this is the first time you create
your solver, ExaHyPE adds this routine into the solver blueprint. If you add
AMR to an existing solver, you have to add the routine manually, as ExaHyPE's
code generator never overwrites a user solver. A simple default
implementation of this routine resembles

    ::exahype2::RefinementCommand MySolver::refinementCriterion(
      const double* const                          Q, // [unknowns+auxiliary]
      const tarch::la::Vector<DIMENSIONS, double>& x,
      const tarch::la::Vector<DIMENSIONS, double>& h,
      const double                                 t
    ) {
      return ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep;
    }

This version does not yet introduce any adaptivity. Your solver returns `Keep`
all the time, so ExaHyPE will create a mesh that just matches @f$h_{\text{max}}@f$
(the coarsest configured resolution) and then keep this match. Once you return
`Refine`, it will however refine. If your mesh however becomes finer
than the configured @f$h_{\text{min}}@f$, ExaHyPE will ignore the refinement request
of your solver.

`Keep` keeps the mesh as it is. That is, if you return `Keep` for an
adaptive grid part, this part will not change. You have to invoke
`Erase` to request coarsening. Both erase and keep are only recommendations; ExaHyPE will
try to meet these requests, but will always make the mesh slightly finer
or not refine if the minimum mesh size would be violated too harshly.
That is, if some parts of the mesh ask for a refinement and direct
neighbours want to coarsen, the refinement gets higher priority. Along
the same lines, erase instructions will simply be ignored if the
resulting mesh would be coarser than @f$h_{\text{max}}@f$.

It is important to keep in mind that refinement requests are not
immediately realised by ExaHyPE. Refinements are requested in one time step,
and then realised in the subsequent one. Also, ExaHyPE will try to accommodate
coarsening requests, but if there are any issues (with mesh partitioning
or balancing, e.g.), then these considerations will have higher
priority and ExaHyPE might decide to continue with a finer mesh than requested.

Different solvers come along with different interpolation and
restriction schemes. The discussion below focuses on Finite Volume solvers.


Interpolation and restriction for Finite Volumes {#section:exahype:finite-volumes:interpolation-schemes}
--------------------------------------------------------

If you employ adaptive mesh refinement, you have to project coarser
resolutions onto finer resolutions (interpolation), and you have to
couple back fine grid solutions to coarser regions of the mesh
(restriction). For this, the Finite Volume solvers employ a
piece-wise constant interpolation and simple averaging as restriction.
Since ExaHyPE2 always divides a cell into three cells, this means that
interpolation simply consists in copying data from the element of the
coarse cell at one third of the index in the fine cell, so that 3^d
elements in the fine cell get the value that was previously in any given
element of the coarse cell.
Equivalently, restriction consists of averaging those fine elements who 
would be contained within the coarse element.

One issue with this scheme is that while it works well with a halo size
of 1, it does not work with halo sizes above 1 as the restriction of
faces requires absent data.
As an example, imagine a finite volume simulation with a halo size of 3:
on the fine face, three fine elements would be present which, together, would
be the same width as one coarse element.
One could fill one of the coarse elements by averaging the values across the
three elements of the fine side, but then one would not have additional data
with which to befill the next two elements in the coarse face.
Alternatively, one could fill each element of the coarse face with one layer
from the fine face, but then one would amplify any gradients present on the
fine face.
Finally, one could fill the first element of the coarse face and then copy
the same data for each subsequent element of the coarse face, but this
misrepresents the data beyond the information that the fine face can actually
provide.

   */
   
